# can_sniffer / can_bringup pinout for UPduino v3
#
# The clock is the board's 12 MHz oscillator, NOT SB_HFOSC, and it only
# reaches the FPGA if jumper R16 ("OSC" on the silkscreen) is shorted.
# Without that jumper there is no clock and the design does nothing.  CAN is
# NRZ with no clock line, so ISO 11898-1 imposes a real accuracy budget that
# the internal oscillator misses by ~10x — see clock_choice.md.
#
# SCK / MOSI / CS keep the same pins uart_to_spi.v and spi_hw_stream.v use, so
# an existing logic-analyzer setup carries over unchanged.  Unlike
# spi_hw_stream the FPGA is the MASTER here and drives all three; there is no
# MISO because the sniffer only ever pushes data out.  These are general
# fabric pins, not the UP5K's dedicated config-SPI pads (14/15/16/17), which
# keeps the onboard flash and the FTDI programmer off this bus; pin 16 is
# still driven high to hold the flash deselected.
#
# dbg_frame is a scope trigger, one pulse per decoded record.  It sits on
# gpio_42 deliberately: that is header position 22, right beside can_rx on
# position 23, so a single scope ground lead reaches both — and it stays well
# clear of the SPI pins.
#
# Numbers below are FPGA pins, which are not header positions:
#   clk_12 20 -> header 44 (leave that position unconnected once OSC is shorted)
#   can_rx 38 -> header 23        dbg_frame 42 -> header 22
#   spi_sck 11 -> header 35       spi_cs 19 -> header 37
#   spi_mosi 21 -> header 39      GND -> header 42

set_io -nowarn led_r        41
set_io -nowarn led_g        39
set_io -nowarn led_b        40

set_io -nowarn spi_cs_flash 16

set_io -nowarn clk_12       20
set_io -nowarn can_rx       38
set_io -nowarn dbg_frame    42

set_io -nowarn spi_sck      11
set_io -nowarn spi_cs       19
set_io -nowarn spi_mosi     21
